/* The header for this test is at the BOTTOM of this file, below the script.
   It is there because a constant inside a leading comment can swallow the
   rest of the file in the engine - see RMSTEST_56 and the directory README.
   Nothing below the script can do any harm. */

<PLAYER_SETUP>
  random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>
  base_terrain WATER

  /* The land. Left quarter of the map, hard edged. */
  create_land
  {
    terrain_type GRASS
    land_percent 22
    base_size 12
    right_border 70
    border_fuzziness 0
  }

  /* The shelf. Immediately seaward of the land, so the three depths run
     land, shallows, open water from left to right. */
  create_land
  {
    terrain_type SHALLOW
    land_percent 18
    base_size 10
    left_border 28
    right_border 50
    border_fuzziness 0
  }

<ELEVATION_GENERATION>


<TERRAIN_GENERATION>


<OBJECTS_GENERATION>


<CONNECTION_GENERATION>

/*
RMSTEST_52 - is the shallows and open-water boundary edged with sand, or only
the land and shallows one?
Feeds: preview-design Sec.6.4, terrains.ts, grid.ts, Sec.15 item 29

QUESTION
The engine paints its own sand at a boundary, with no command asking for it.
Sec.6.4 now treats shallows as their OWN DEPTH rather than as water, so the rule
is that a tile takes its beach wherever it borders anything strictly deeper than
itself, and sand lands on the shallower side. That produces two edges around a
shallows shelf running out to sea: sand between the land and the shelf, and sand
between the shelf and the open water.

The near edge is not in doubt. Shallows already read as water before this change
and that boundary was already being edged - incidentally, and for the wrong
reason, which is how the shallows the terrain table calls dry ended up with no
beach at all.

THE FAR EDGE IS THE CLAIM AND IT IS UNMEASURED.

What supports it: the terrain-state entry documents a flag for "thinner blending
of shallows and beach terrain", which is a statement that the two are adjacent
by default rather than only when an author puts them together. And a shelf whose
seaward edge is bare looks wrong beside one whose landward edge is not.

What is NOT evidence, and was checked: a corpus map paints its own beach terrain
over every shallow it owns with a cosmetic mask. That could be an author
supplying a beach the engine does not, or an author choosing a different beach
from the one it does. The file cannot distinguish them, which is the whole
reason this needs a run.

METHOD
Open water base, a grass land on the left, and a shallows band laid as its own
land immediately to its right so the three depths sit in a known left-to-right
order: land, shallows, open water. Read the two boundaries.

The shallows band is a LAND rather than a terrain command on purpose. A terrain
command would scatter shallows wherever it found water and might not touch the
land at all, and the whole reading depends on the three depths being adjacent in
a known order.

  sand on BOTH boundaries -> the shipped model is right. Strike the marker.
  sand only against the land -> the far half is wrong. The fix is one flag, not
                                a rebuild: shallows stop being their own depth
                                for the seaward comparison and the near edge
                                keeps working as it already did.
  sand on neither -> the map is wrong, not the model. Most likely the two lands
                     did not end up adjacent. Check the bounding boxes before
                     concluding anything.

Also read WHICH sand. The shipped answer is each shallow's own beach terrain -
ordinary sand for the ordinary shallows, the icy variant for navigable ice -
matching the per-terrain rule already measured on the land side. If both edges
come back with the same sand regardless of the shallow, that is a separate
finding and it is worth more than the boundary question.

GENERATE AT NORMAL (200), any player count. TWO RUNS.

READ IT WITH
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --patches BEACH
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --patches SHALLOW
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --patches GRASS
  Read the bounding boxes and compare the sand strips against the two known
  boundaries. A strip on the land side only is the refutation; strips on both is
  the model.
*/
